WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.090
Film Courage: We’re going to start with
some questions that we got from Youtube.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:09.680
This one is from Kay.Puta and Kay writes “How
were you able to juggle a production company

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.340
through high school and University and still
be a comedy legend?”

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.269
Todd Berger, Co-Writer of THE HAPPYTIME MURDERS:
Wow?

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:16.490
That’s all news to me.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.100
Film Courage: Thank you Kay.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.660
Todd: Comedy legend?

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:26.539
When I was a kid I started my first production
company when I was nine or ten Boy of Destiny

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:29.220
Productions.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:35.100
It was making movies, making little videos
with my friends, it was my hobby.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:43.149
Like when other kids were playing soccer or
kissing girls, like I was making little movies

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:44.149
with friends.

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:49.070
And as I got older and got out of high school
I found fellow friends who wanted to make

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:55.800
movies and we formed a production company,
even just…we made a feature in high school,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.440
I made three features by the time we went
to college over the summers.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:12.009
And from a young age I knew that I wanted
to work in creative entertainment fields and

00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:14.889
I couldn’t imagine doing anything else.

00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:21.590
So starting as a company that started as a
goof and then actually Johnny Voodoo productions

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:28.060
which I started as a goof in high school and
it now my actual LLC production company that

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:35.899
I used to make stuff (I’ve just maintained
it ever since) and yeah I guess I’ve just

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:45.149
always kept that going because I have jobs
to make money but also for fun or for a creative

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:50.649
artistic outlet there is this stuff I do over
hear and luckily we live in an industry, we

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:53.719
work in an industry where you can sometimes
make those things overlap.

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:57.009
I don’t know if that answered your question
at all?

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:01.229
Film Courage: I think I does…it was actually
Kay’s question but I think that does.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:03.609
You’ve talked about how you grew up in…sorry
Nawlins’?

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:05.799
Todd: It’s New Orleans.

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:07.990
We don’t like ‘Nawlins.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.379
When you spell it Nawlins…no.

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.430
Film Courage: Sorry…just trying to be cool.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:13.430
See it backfired.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.700
So you were born and raised in New Orleans.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.120
Todd: Born and raised in New Orleans on the
Bayou…not really.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:25.959
But I grew up on the other side of the Mississippi
River like from the French Quarter and like

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:32.150
growing up in New Orleans was…I didn’t
realize how unique it was while I was growing

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.530
up because I just thought every city was like
this.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:40.440
It’s like “Every city has got…is super
old and you have Mardi Gras, right?”

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.150
I didn’t realize, I just didn’t understand
it’s uniqueness until I left and I was like

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.120
“Oh, that’s a pretty cool place.”

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:54.550
And the whole city has a vibe of creative
energy and really supporting creativity and

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:55.550
I love it.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.609
Film Courage: And there’s like a supernatural,
mediumship undercurrent.

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:00.939
Todd: Oh yeah, there’s a lot of voodoo.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:02.660
There’s a lot of ghosts.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:03.689
Everything is haunted.

00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:06.799
Everywhere you go there is some spirit doing
something.

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:08.160
Film Courage: Nice.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.160
Hopefully behaving?

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.329
Todd: Usually pretty good.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:17.670
Well like the cool thing about New Orleans
is every where you go it’s like “See that

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.590
house over there, it’s like a 180 years
old and a bunch of people died in that house.”

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.599
And you’re like “Oh, okay cool.”

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:26.430
But now it’s a dentist’s office.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.010
Film Courage: Yeah, sure.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.079
Now he does root canals.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:35.840
Okay so now we have another question from
Nate’s Film Tutorials “How did you go

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.020
about starting your own production company
and what advice would you give to those who

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.020
are starting out?

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.020
(I’m 19 and decided not to go to film school,
but to learn myself).

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.020
So we kind of talked about that?

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.540
Todd: Yes, all you have to do is start a production
company is register it with the city and then

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.790
you have a company and then it’s like you
actually have to go do stuff, you have to

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:57.180
make stuff.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.769
I live in Los Angeles and no matter where
you live you find the creative people in your

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:07.019
city and you kind of like hang out with them
and get to know them and become friends with

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:16.389
them and it’s amazing even going to a barbecue,
like it’s a friend’s birthday barbecue

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:19.889
and you go and you start talking to people
and you’re like “So what do you do?”

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:21.539
“I’m a composer.”

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:22.639
“Oh, cool?

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:26.500
I actually have been looking for a composer.”

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.650
And then you get their card and you get their
information and the next time you need a composer,

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.080
you met that person at a barbecue.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:39.819
And they often say in the movie business it’s
Who You Know but that doesn’t mean your

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:40.819
family connections.

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:46.300
It’s just like who you know socially in
life because you went to a barbecue and met

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:52.780
a composer and then part of having a production
company or making stuff is knowing people

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.150
in all fields.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:01.440
It’s not just…as a writer I sit at home
working on my laptop by myself.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.490
But if you want to actually produce stuff
and have a production company, you need to

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.639
know people in all fields.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:11.560
You need to know sound people, you need to
know composers, you need to know craft services

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.919
people, you have to just get out and be introduced
to people in the fields so that when it comes

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:24.560
time to make something if you have a production
company, you need to be able to call anyone

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.220
in any position and if someone is like “Hey
do you have a transpo guy?

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.620
Someone who drives trucks?”

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.669
You’re like “Yeah, I know three.

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:34.340
I know three people I can call right now.”

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.550
And that is really helpful.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.610
Film Courage: Do you have to force yourself
to get out sometimes?

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.080
Todd: Oh yeah.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.449
I can be an isolationist often.

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:48.300
But also just trying to get on set in any
capacity.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.610
When I was younger I was just a PA on set
and you just meet everybody.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:57.419
You meet the craft services person, you meet
the transpo guys, you meet the hair and make-up

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:02.259
people and you become friendly with them and
you become friendly with them.

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:11.250
So much of production is just referrals of
the DP is working on a new project and that

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:17.009
project needs a craft services and he’s
like “Oh, well I know a person I met on

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:18.170
my last thing.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:22.139
Here…why don’t you call them?”

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:23.680
I love being on set.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.050
I love directing and being on set.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.639
That’s so much fun that to me that’s almost
like networking right?

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:33.970
Is just being on a set meeting everybody.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:40.819
Some directors will go on set and not talk
to anybody but their AD or the actors.

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:44.340
But I’m someone like on the first day I
try to introduce myself to everybody.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.380
I want to know who the transpo guys are, I
want to know every art department person.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.080
What’s there name and get to know them all
and become Facebook friends with them because

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.040
maybe we’ll work together.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.389
Maybe I’ll need you or you’ll need me?

00:06:58.389 --> 00:06:58.710
Who knows?

